WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:03.086
The earliest
humans didn't have words for colors.

00:00:03.086 --> 00:00:05.588
They had words for objects and actions.

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:08.800
And it took tens of thousands of years
for those words

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.803
to evolve into the names of the colors
we use today.

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:14.973
Looking back, gives us a glimpse
at how those early people saw

00:00:14.973 --> 00:00:16.433
the world around them.

00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:20.061
We'll also answer that age old question:
which came first?

00:00:20.145 --> 00:00:22.856
Orange the fruit, or orange the color?

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:24.232
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky

00:00:24.232 --> 00:00:26.026
and this is Otherwords.

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:37.370
The human eye

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.373
can perceive millions
of slight gradations of color.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.752
But unless you're a designer or an artist,
you probably

00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:46.755
only regularly use
about ten or so color words.

00:00:46.963 --> 00:00:49.966
And they are remarkably similar across
all cultures.

00:00:50.091 --> 00:00:54.763
A landmark study by Brent Berlin and Paul
Kay found that people across the world

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:59.434
developed their words for colors in more
or less the same chronological order.

00:00:59.642 --> 00:01:00.685
For example,

00:01:00.685 --> 00:01:04.856
if a language only had two words for
colors, they were always black and white.

00:01:04.939 --> 00:01:09.402
If a language had only three color words,
they were black, white and red.

00:01:09.486 --> 00:01:14.324
Yellow and green came next in either
order, then blue, brown and so on.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:17.869
This hierarchy closely matches
human psychology.

00:01:17.952 --> 00:01:21.331
After all,
what could be more visually fundamental

00:01:21.331 --> 00:01:24.209
than the dichotomy
between light and darkness?

00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:28.546
And we're hardwired to have
a strong emotional reaction to red,

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:33.259
as it has a lot of survival significance
from food to sex to violence.

00:01:33.301 --> 00:01:34.928
Blue is surprisingly far

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:38.014
down the list, considering it's
most people's favorite color.

00:01:38.014 --> 00:01:41.518
But actually due to a chemical quirk,

00:01:41.518 --> 00:01:45.980
blue is very rare in nature, except,
of course, for the sky.

00:01:46.064 --> 00:01:49.567
But you don't really need a word
to help identify the sky.

00:01:49.651 --> 00:01:51.194
Which sky do you mean?

00:01:51.194 --> 00:01:53.279
Oh, the blue sky!

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:54.697
The English word white

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:59.285
can be traced all the way back
to the Proto-Indo-European root kweit

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.121
Which meant to shine.

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:04.749
Black similarly goes back to the P.I.E. bhleg,

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:09.254
which meant to burn, a reference
to the color of what's left after burning

00:02:09.337 --> 00:02:13.424
red is unique in that it's
the only color that has a P.I.E. root

00:02:13.424 --> 00:02:16.427
that just meant the same thing: red.

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:21.766
Although it may have been used to describe
anything that had a warm or interesting color.

00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:26.062
Yellow came from the P.I.E. ghel,
which also meant to shine

00:02:26.271 --> 00:02:31.067
and gave us many related words
like gold, glimmer, glow and gleam.

00:02:31.151 --> 00:02:34.821
Green comes from the P.I.E. ghre,
which meant to grow

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:39.742
for its obvious connection to plants
and also gave us graze and grass.

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:43.454
Interestingly, even though humans
can perceive more shades of green

00:02:43.454 --> 00:02:47.625
than any other color,
we just use one word for them all.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:48.751
Think about it.

00:02:48.751 --> 00:02:52.088
How many different kinds of red
can you name compared to different kinds

00:02:52.088 --> 00:02:52.881
of green?

00:02:52.881 --> 00:02:55.925
The reason is probably
that even though our ancestors

00:02:55.925 --> 00:02:59.888
were surrounded by green, or rather
because they were surrounded by green,

00:02:59.971 --> 00:03:02.182
it's just not that interesting to us.

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:04.809
A 2016 study by Hannah J. Haynie

00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:08.646
and Claire Bowern found that humans
across many different languages

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:12.984
have an easier time communicating
warm colors than cool colors.

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:14.569
This color wheel, for instance,

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:18.990
has twice as many common color
words on the warm side than the cool side.

00:03:19.073 --> 00:03:20.617
To understand why,

00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:24.662
just picture with the world
may have looked like to our early ancestors.

00:03:24.746 --> 00:03:27.373
Which things in their field of view
were worth talking about?

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:30.376
The cool colored ones
or the warm colored ones?

00:03:30.585 --> 00:03:33.463
Perhaps for this reason,
the word blue has been traced

00:03:33.463 --> 00:03:37.508
back to the P.I.E. root bhle,
which meant yellow.

00:03:37.592 --> 00:03:41.721
There simply wasn't a need to describe
the color blue until much later on

00:03:41.971 --> 00:03:46.392
when humans started making dyes and paints
from rare materials like lapis lazuli.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.770
Orange was pretty late to the scene.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.856
The old English term for the color
between yellow and red

00:03:51.856 --> 00:03:55.360
was geoluhread, 
which literally meant yellow-red.

00:03:55.443 --> 00:04:00.156
But then sometime around the 15th
or 16th century, Portuguese merchants

00:04:00.156 --> 00:04:05.370
began importing an exotic fruit to Europe,
known by its Sanskrit name, naranga.

00:04:05.453 --> 00:04:07.914
This came to be known naranja in Spanish,

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:11.376
arancia in Italian, and orange in French.

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:14.921
The latter two likely dropped
the N at the beginning through confusion

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:16.881
about where the article ended.

00:04:16.881 --> 00:04:22.595
It's the same way we got an apron from
a napron and a nickname from an eke name.

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:26.808
Over the next couple hundred years,
people went from saying things were orange

00:04:26.808 --> 00:04:30.812
colored or the color of an orange
to just saying they were orange.

00:04:31.062 --> 00:04:34.065
So there you have it.
The fruit came first.

00:04:34.148 --> 00:04:37.402
But we're still living with the effects
of not having a proper name

00:04:37.402 --> 00:04:39.612
for the color for a long time.

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:43.658
It's why this is called a redhead
and this a red robin,

00:04:43.825 --> 00:04:46.786
even though they're both clearly the color
of an orange.

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:52.875
Brown comes from the P.I.E. bher, which meant bright,
possibly in the sense of polished wood.

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:56.212
Indeed, it also gives us the word
burnished.

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:58.798
The animal
that we today call a bear went by a

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:01.801
totally different word
prior to the Middle Ages.

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:06.097
But since it was considered dangerous to
even mention the fearsome beast by name,

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.727
ancient hunters came up with the euphemism:
bero, which meant brown one.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.146
They were so superstitious, in fact,

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:17.150
that the true old English word for bear
is now lost to history.

00:05:17.233 --> 00:05:21.487
Purple is one of the few common
color words without a root.

00:05:21.571 --> 00:05:25.366
It comes from the Latin purpura,
which referred to a certain shellfish

00:05:25.366 --> 00:05:28.745
that was ground down to make
a distinctly colored dye.

00:05:28.745 --> 00:05:30.913
Because of its vibrance and rarity,

00:05:30.913 --> 00:05:32.540
Purple became very popular

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:36.669
with the wealthy and powerful and
is still associated with royalty today.

00:05:36.753 --> 00:05:39.756
It wasn't really until humans
became proficient artists

00:05:39.922 --> 00:05:43.551
that we started adding colors
to our vocabulary by the hundreds.

00:05:43.634 --> 00:05:48.890
Most are either based on the source
of the pigment, like stones, plants

00:05:48.973 --> 00:05:52.393
and insects,
or to what the color reminds us of,

00:05:52.393 --> 00:05:56.481
whether it's the sky,
the birds or a beverage.

00:05:56.564 --> 00:05:59.025
So how many unique color names are there?

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:04.113
It's hard to say, but it's definitely
in the hundreds, if not thousands.

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:08.326
Some have long histories dating back
to the beginning of civilization,

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:11.704
and some were made up recently
by crayon marketing departments.

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:15.583
You may not need more than ten or
so on a day to day basis,

00:06:15.583 --> 00:06:19.420
but the explosion of words shows
that color has gone from something

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:22.507
that we just used to survive
to an integral part

00:06:22.507 --> 00:06:32.100
of expressing ourselves
and making our imaginations a reality.

00:06:32.183 --> 00:06:35.395
What could
be more visually fundamental than the--

00:06:35.436 --> 00:06:37.522
Then the dichotomy...

00:06:37.522 --> 00:06:40.191
considering it's 
most people's favorite color.

